# 69. Project Governance

---

# 1. Purpose

Project Governance defines the decision-making structure, responsibilities, approval process and operational rules for the Loyalty Platform project.

Its objective is to ensure that Product, Architecture, Engineering, QA, DevOps and AI implementation remain aligned throughout the entire project lifecycle.

Governance protects the Blueprint from uncontrolled changes while enabling continuous delivery.

---

# 2. Scope

Project Governance applies to:

- Product Decisions
- Architecture
- Engineering
- AI Agents
- Source Code
- Documentation
- Infrastructure
- Security
- Releases
- Production Operations

Every participant in the project follows this governance model.

---

# 3. Governance Principles

The project is governed by the following principles:

- Product First
- Blueprint is Authoritative
- Architecture Before Implementation
- Single Source of Truth
- Explicit Ownership
- Controlled Change
- Documentation Synchronization
- AI-Assisted Development
- Traceability by Default
- Continuous Improvement

---

# 4. Governance Structure

The project governance structure consists of:

```
Product Owner

↓

Solution Architect

↓

Project Manager

↓

Engineering Leads

↓

Implementation Agents

↓

QA

↓

Release Manager

↓

Production Operations
```

Each role has clearly defined responsibilities.

---

# 5. Product Owner Responsibilities

The Product Owner owns:

- Product Vision
- Business Priorities
- Product Decisions
- Backlog Prioritization
- Feature Acceptance
- Business Scope
- Business Value

Only the Product Owner may approve new Product Decisions.

---

# 6. Solution Architect Responsibilities

The Solution Architect owns:

- Architecture
- Domain Model
- Module Boundaries
- APIs
- Events
- Technical Direction
- Module Implementation Packages
- ADR Approval

The Solution Architect protects architectural consistency.

---

# 7. Project Manager Responsibilities

The Project Manager owns:

- planning;
- scheduling;
- task assignment;
- dependency management;
- delivery tracking;
- implementation coordination.

The Project Manager does not approve architectural changes.

---

# 8. Engineering Responsibilities

Engineering teams own:

- implementation;
- code quality;
- testing;
- documentation updates;
- implementation evidence;
- technical debt reporting.

Engineering does not change Product Decisions.

---

# 9. QA Responsibilities

QA owns:

- validation;
- regression testing;
- UAT preparation;
- production verification;
- release quality.

QA verifies behavior rather than implementation.

---

# 10. DevOps Responsibilities

DevOps owns:

- infrastructure;
- CI/CD;
- deployments;
- monitoring;
- backups;
- disaster recovery;
- operational tooling.

DevOps does not bypass Release Governance.

---

# 11. Documentation Responsibilities

Documentation ownership includes:

- Blueprint
- Engineering Playbook
- ADRs
- Module Implementation Packages
- Task documentation
- Release documentation

Documentation must remain synchronized with implementation.

---

# 12. AI Agent Responsibilities

AI Agents are responsible for:

- implementing assigned tasks;
- following Coding Standards;
- respecting module boundaries;
- producing implementation evidence;
- updating documentation when required.

AI Agents may suggest improvements but may not redefine project governance.

---

# 13. Decision Types

Project decisions fall into four categories.

## Product Decisions

Owned by:

Product Owner

---

## Architecture Decisions

Owned by:

Solution Architect

Documented as ADRs.

---

## Engineering Decisions

Owned by:

Engineering Leads

Must remain compatible with Blueprint and ADRs.

---

## Operational Decisions

Owned by:

Release Manager / DevOps

---

# 14. Change Management

Every change must belong to one of the following:

- Product Change
- Architecture Change
- Engineering Improvement
- Defect Correction
- Operational Improvement

Each category follows its own approval process.

---

# 15. Change Approval Matrix

| Change | Approval Required |
|---------|-------------------|
| Product Rule | Product Owner |
| Architecture | Solution Architect |
| API Contract | Solution Architect |
| Event Contract | Solution Architect |
| Database Schema | Solution Architect + Database Review |
| Security | Security Review |
| Release | Release Manager |
| Documentation | Documentation Review |

Critical changes may require multiple approvals.

---

# 16. Escalation Rules

Escalation is required when:

- Blueprint ambiguity is discovered;
- conflicting Product Decisions exist;
- architecture cannot satisfy requirements;
- implementation blocks multiple modules;
- production risk is identified.

Escalation should occur before implementation continues.

---

# 17. Documentation Governance

Documentation changes follow these rules:

- Blueprint changes require Product approval.
- Engineering changes require Architecture approval.
- ADR changes require a new ADR.
- Historical documents are never rewritten.
- Authoritative documents remain the implementation reference.

---

# 18. AI Governance

AI implementation must always:

- use approved Knowledge Packages;
- follow Module Implementation Packages;
- remain inside task scope;
- produce implementation evidence;
- submit work for review.

AI Agents must never:

- invent Product Decisions;
- redefine architecture;
- bypass review;
- modify unrelated modules.

---

# 19. Quality Gates

Every implementation passes through mandatory quality gates.

Minimum gates include:

- Definition of Task Ready
- Implementation
- Self Verification
- Technical Review
- QA
- Merge
- Release
- Production Verification

Skipping a quality gate is prohibited.

---

# 20. Risk Governance

Project risks are classified as:

| Level | Description |
|--------|-------------|
| R1 | Low |
| R2 | Moderate |
| R3 | High |
| R4 | Critical |

Every High or Critical risk must have:

- owner;
- mitigation plan;
- review date;
- current status.

---

# 21. Exception Process

When governance cannot be followed:

1. document the exception;
2. identify affected modules;
3. identify associated risks;
4. obtain approval;
5. record follow-up actions.

Exceptions should remain temporary.

---

# 22. Audit Process

Regular audits should verify:

- Blueprint compliance;
- Engineering compliance;
- Coding Standards;
- documentation consistency;
- module ownership;
- security;
- production readiness.

Audit findings should be tracked until resolved.

---

# 23. Governance Metrics

Recommended governance metrics include:

Planning

- Ready Tasks
- Blocked Tasks

Quality

- Review Pass Rate
- Documentation Drift
- Defect Escape Rate

Architecture

- ADR Compliance
- Module Boundary Violations

Operations

- Deployment Success Rate
- Rollback Frequency
- Incident Count

Governance

- Unapproved Changes
- Documentation Coverage
- Task Traceability

---

# 24. Governance Reviews

Formal governance reviews should occur:

- before every major release;
- after every production incident;
- after significant architectural changes;
- at the end of each implementation milestone.

Review outcomes should generate improvement actions where necessary.

---

# 25. Definition of Project Governance Ready

Project Governance is considered ready when:

- ownership is clearly defined;
- approval responsibilities are defined;
- change management is defined;
- documentation governance is defined;
- AI governance is defined;
- quality gates are defined;
- escalation process is defined;
- audit process is defined;
- governance metrics are defined.

This document becomes the authoritative governance framework for the Loyalty Platform project after Blueprint Freeze v1.0.